simduino_leonardo is an Arduino Leonardo: an ATmega32U4 at 16MHz running
Arduino's Caterina bootloader, with the board's USB port exported over
USB/IP. Once a USB/IP client attaches it, it is a Leonardo plugged into
the host, and avrdude, the Arduino IDE or arduino-cli upload sketches to
it the same way.

	make
	./obj-*/simduino_leonardo.elf

Linux
-----
The client is the mainline vhci-hcd driver (USBIP_VHCI_HCD in the kernel
configuration) and the usbip tool, packaged as usbip, linux-tools or
net-misc/usbip depending on the distribution:

	sudo modprobe vhci-hcd
	usbip list -r 127.0.0.1
	sudo usbip attach -r 127.0.0.1 -b 1-1

The bootloader shows up as 2341:0036 and a /dev/ttyACM device, which
avrdude programs:

	avrdude -c avr109 -p m32u4 -P /dev/ttyACM0 -U flash:w:sketch.hex

"sudo usbip port" lists attached devices and "sudo usbip detach -p
<port>" removes one.

If opening the port fails with an I/O error, and the kernel log shows the
board being reset and reconnected under a new device number, USB
autosuspend is enabled (power/control is "auto", often set by a power
management tool). Caterina is built without the answer to GET_STATUS for
the device, which Linux asks for when it resumes a suspended device; it
then resets the board and takes it for a new one. A real Leonardo in its
bootloader does the same. Turn autosuspend off for devices that appear
from then on, until the next boot:

	echo -1 | sudo tee /sys/module/usbcore/parameters/autosuspend

or for the attached board only, until it re-enumerates:

	echo on | sudo tee /sys/bus/usb/devices/<port>/power/control

Windows
-------
Build simavr in MSYS2 as usual. The client is usbip-win2
(https://github.com/vadimgrn/usbip-win2, or "winget install
vadimgrn.usbip-win2"), Windows 10 1903 or later. From an administrator
prompt:

	usbip list -r 127.0.0.1
	usbip attach -r 127.0.0.1 -b 1-1

The board then has a COM port.

Resets and reattaching
----------------------
As on the board, the flash starts erased, and Caterina waits in the
bootloader until it is given a sketch. After a power-on with a sketch in
the flash, Caterina starts it at once. After the reset button, it waits
8 seconds for an upload first. -r starts the simulation as after the
reset button, and Ctrl-\ presses it while it runs (not on Windows). A
sketch built with the Arduino core resets into the bootloader when the
port is opened at 1200 baud and closed, the usual way to upload, through
a watchdog reset.

Each time the firmware disconnects from the bus, when the bootloader
starts the sketch, at a reset, or when a sketch is reset into the
bootloader, the USB/IP connection is closed and the board has to be
attached again, which is when a real board re-enumerates.

The flash is kept in simduino_leonardo_flash.bin in the current directory
and written back when simduino_leonardo stops (Ctrl-C included), so the
next run finds the sketch uploaded last. -F names another file, -n starts
erased and keeps nothing. A sketch given on the command line, .hex or
.elf, is programmed over it at start.

simduino_leonardo.elf -h lists the options. The server only listens on
127.0.0.1 unless given another address with -a; "-a 0.0.0.0" lets other
machines attach the board.

The bootloader
--------------
Caterina-Leonardo-bootloader.hex is Arduino's Caterina-Leonardo.hex with
the records below 0x7000 removed, which hold a demonstration sketch, so
that only the bootloader remains, at 0x7000 to 0x7FD9. See LICENSE.

USB/IP: https://docs.kernel.org/usb/usbip_protocol.html
